#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a00c0b-29bc-5b57-873f-d3261312d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431311} {:answer/id #uuid "8679841f-eada-5a6a-a61b-82efd37aeeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431315} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9b61b-3964-54cb-9363-7eec9cb93295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431316} {:answer/id #uuid "caeafa19-d4ce-5934-ae44-0b3839cecee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431332} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c2bd4b-edba-50d5-a801-73a966ee31b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64a00c0b-29bc-5b57-873f-d3261312d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d54ccd3-fe03-5cf8-b3a3-a6a1f53e813a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f71d9a8-f991-5b25-b40f-81e743cd06de" :question/math-academy-id "q-126864" :question/problem "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-9+8 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} {:answer/id #uuid "2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431292} {:answer/id #uuid "3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431298} {:answer/id #uuid "750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431312} {:answer/id #uuid "8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126855" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220be106-05bc-5093-b00a-7db60bd8ecaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431288} {:answer/id #uuid "37256756-976d-5c9b-9621-726f21d7d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431295} {:answer/id #uuid "3add5703-5575-518a-8fb6-785fcc0ae348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431297} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c51c42-3a40-510d-8c2c-1c2f6b8e0682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431327} {:answer/id #uuid "ddae96ae-32de-50a4-bd74-13c0f92807b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddae96ae-32de-50a4-bd74-13c0f92807b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431334} :answer-field/id #uuid "800a953b-6548-5c1f-a6b5-8f730ae36aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6cffb2-428d-5a69-9a22-1115ee05f052" :question/math-academy-id "q-126891" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e71bc835596d8699688f3623f339c26d365a8bb6e888742cbea10fc9da722adc.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}5y\\,dx+11x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=5y$ and $Q(x,y)=11x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11-5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 14\\pi  \\\\  & =84\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236daf0a-8fec-5986-adef-4fec2089883e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431289} {:answer/id #uuid "2980d347-4cd0-508a-a19e-609126e74ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431291} {:answer/id #uuid "b95f6d7e-5d5f-5174-809f-affea38f8fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431328} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3bde0-ed52-52bf-9585-a17e40e4ed16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431329} {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2034a12-3dc0-57c4-b9a4-d7673fd61b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431336} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b281059-95fd-5ff3-8dbf-e28f1dfbb9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" :question/math-academy-id "q-126938" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0918efff-3716-5e79-bc16-ce01b647dee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431283} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4ab89c-0bd1-5d9d-bbfe-405c2f326073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431284} {:answer/id #uuid "b158c6b6-52a5-5218-9685-8e39e2b380c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431323} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f98841-106f-5d7f-bf0f-b27f11e0da85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} {:answer/id #uuid "fe684fde-4550-539f-a64b-9a955faeea4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f98841-106f-5d7f-bf0f-b27f11e0da85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431324} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb80ec8-d8a5-5f3b-808d-db46b94db516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2fd9b28-2de8-59c7-8e8c-46e14a79f680" :question/math-academy-id "q-126880" :question/problem "![](images/65/65047f9bf4f035000175fc55ee3ba6eeb4aa7ec39674a1984b95ba98a3f754f6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=13x.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(13-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (18-4) \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f39220d-1633-509c-81a8-5131d70771c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431293} {:answer/id #uuid "39f62df8-11d8-553d-8fdd-204067835d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431296} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} {:answer/id #uuid "59acde88-fd8f-54be-a537-e285b05463de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431307} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef94c4-e6c8-5f82-b55a-2a267716a18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431299} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d5399-1389-5996-9269-7e769b0bb606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d01c58ab-6639-5fe1-a3d3-01aeb78816a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126942" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbe6009d42623d3b3cd9f05380159bb0f4b8f22b920c1ef5d4d82b5dbf152d4a.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y(x+1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6xy+2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6xy+2y)-2y(x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-0)\\cdot (4-1)-(4-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d37215b-1943-5905-97aa-565540294dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431300} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b7de02-99e4-5e8d-9b2d-adc7d46bc083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431320} {:answer/id #uuid "a55fde2d-708a-592b-8240-a4b285208e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431321} {:answer/id #uuid "adc8174a-05b0-5d22-bae6-7c69965d1aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431322} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b1df00-1a0c-583b-9b5b-65ba53909dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0020cb-0a2c-5126-8edf-5125f13da402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f20c5355-cea7-5f07-9f8f-ed2bfe376f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-126946" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9be396fd57559bb77f92d5a0421f3a7cdca577203904e577da0caf5326caac91.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x$ and $Q(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-8{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))\\cdot (16-16)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8f90a5-b39b-592d-a1d9-ab32a0cec7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431287} {:answer/id #uuid "462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} {:answer/id #uuid "61820d09-1fb4-5a1a-a196-e88b05cc9b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431309} {:answer/id #uuid "62afb4f8-e3ba-552d-b94f-0f8ee6e59d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431310} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bbdd2-bf0b-5780-9c2e-2d82fda1acee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431303} :answer-field/id #uuid "23144688-cc3e-5691-a60e-f350dd3406bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa88e59c-b562-550a-9e35-b904fd919d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126861" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433350} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9da3b51-53f9-58fc-b00c-db0ea43214f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62968591-e72a-5306-bd3f-0e762f4b0368" :question/math-academy-id "q-353352" :question/problem "![](images/03/0353d9a2b8c8e46552e0816658628f67b58c0c6bef51bd05261becac42a55798.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,-2<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{t+2}\\right)}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{0}(0+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1)(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433769} {:answer/id #uuid "8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433771} {:answer/id #uuid "bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433774} {:answer/id #uuid "d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433776} {:answer/id #uuid "df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433776} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-b73eead72363" :question/math-academy-id "q-83975" :question/problem "![](images/37/37a6eb13f8a6f6ab46040495894dedeec4951b4814aad19f45bfbd4ed7040767.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433768} {:answer/id #uuid "8c54d999-f74e-5c26-b531-09a8860a231c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433770} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce35c76-148f-5bb3-a99f-541b58a26f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433772} {:answer/id #uuid "a0af3adb-9d86-553c-acba-e8f816cbacfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433773} {:answer/id #uuid "be87cc15-09cf-5525-b687-f5d34bc230e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433768} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7d38f6-a7fa-5865-98e5-ea6506d22a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9675b0c7-9ed4-51ad-88df-5786551b6bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-83998" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fca2d0c37c8831a66c3c183b31b84b41fd5a16740ff13fd86f4877bdaeb60a6.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/10/1060c071df1dbd197c14da529819a34bd4457868fdd4cbe78a7f93f12dbf67da.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (2-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000302c2-3c84-5577-bb85-c11cc3bf9cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436008} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb60a10-e100-54a3-a098-70fb2ea763e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436010} {:answer/id #uuid "6df11da8-be9b-5344-b62d-79a4559efa3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436014} {:answer/id #uuid "a513aedd-cbc7-5326-bb5e-498f3f229294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436016} {:answer/id #uuid "df1cdfb6-d415-5196-8163-5b0778c79a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000302c2-3c84-5577-bb85-c11cc3bf9cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436008} :answer-field/id #uuid "30fcae88-a1e2-5970-b696-299270bb46fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "563f43ed-8e03-5890-8ba0-fe3a95b9c80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128450" :question/problem "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(-2,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,-1) & =4{(-2)}^{2}+9{(-1)}^{2} \\\\  & =4(4)+9(1) \\\\  & =16+9 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the $25$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=25.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(-2,-1)$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0329b153-bf57-5eea-8e4f-f3f01337857b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436009} {:answer/id #uuid "44caf6b5-57f9-522a-a950-aba744b9e3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436012} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6f7295-9d6c-517b-9e6e-6ee1b2c02c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436013} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0b0721-1fd0-5994-a36a-82e40db9769c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436015} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8a8126-4229-53cd-bece-781ab226e64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a0b0721-1fd0-5994-a36a-82e40db9769c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436015} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ea0465-98a8-532d-bb2d-293a976cf74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af624627-f936-5c7e-a5ee-772c5d7da48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-128448" :question/problem "What is the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(3,-1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{x-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =\\frac{2}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the $\\frac{1}{2}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4 & =x-y \\\\ y & =x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(3,-1)$ is the line $y=x-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19118c24-d5da-5954-9e93-75cfa8bacbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436011} {:answer/id #uuid "b68c227f-79b4-539e-9f21-17aa72aae1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3fde41d8d88f0d843e80ae813b07ec030502bcbaa060bc5d54ade4f5207a8740.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436017} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fdc308-acd9-599d-98f4-61b51923dcf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/ca10c21bb658bc3075d0d66321d888c2b23596c1416f605ab0bb8cf79c40af24.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436019} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4ea415-1a42-5ab9-a4f6-74d5d9c47e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/0857b5d843c6d3f5ca6218ee0f27fe169aedc59676b245578bc8db21fabe6178.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436021} {:answer/id #uuid "f049c9a7-cc49-5c3f-a4df-0714b2f9933a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c6a799db8c86ff2bfb702dbba92f8d26011d33016aa33f5df1b4a58601205f04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19118c24-d5da-5954-9e93-75cfa8bacbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436011} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf8aa6a-75ee-5eec-b556-6b1e3d610f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5ccf563-8a57-5f1f-ad06-ef72dc726ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-129114" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [0,\\infty ),$ the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c44ae1b59af6ac6f64efedcf4242aaad07ddcf98c2b63c4aeae7958a6622874.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f9894f-9b1a-5900-9728-e89ee154ca0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{254\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436636} {:answer/id #uuid "68aa1ac1-e8a7-508d-88c0-b565a4e94a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccd92ca-3dd3-51a1-b84b-9d383e7fe955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{255\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436638} {:answer/id #uuid "91f7c777-0592-5298-8f5a-8cd55fbdab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{510\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436639} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8af1eb-e88d-5f7f-b4bb-c743c4774a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68aa1ac1-e8a7-508d-88c0-b565a4e94a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{508\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ba2466-990e-5f7d-96e7-58f6c3675c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50c86907-f8d2-5518-873d-192b1784708d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161119" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16e4559960b923535cadecb1e623592c07fa11de5929e722cffb85679195a66.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}^{3/4}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}({r}^{2})^{3/4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3/2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{2}{7}{r}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(128-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{508\\pi }{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1d4e45-804b-5781-a8d5-a26e64d5d107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436825} {:answer/id #uuid "47b586ec-b7a1-51dc-aebe-7c3c8e874aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436830} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9314df-764a-5a13-88af-6d3fa98e5570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436834} {:answer/id #uuid "ced8cb93-fea7-5255-adf7-bbf81df0fb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436841} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee39a5-5e9c-56c4-a239-5bf26581cedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced8cb93-fea7-5255-adf7-bbf81df0fb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436841} :answer-field/id #uuid "50946ad3-7b33-5f99-8c2f-0f86e0968171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d6554d4-4442-5e1f-83e6-be1fca151acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20067" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨2,-5,7⟩$ and $a=⟨8,-2,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨8,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-5,7⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+(-4)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =16+10-28 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c868e06-3d4f-58da-ac1c-394649473371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436823} {:answer/id #uuid "8f647f5b-f844-5f83-a3b5-4dd41251ce53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436833} {:answer/id #uuid "a5827dcf-e234-5aca-a530-b57e41c8cee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436836} {:answer/id #uuid "cc450cf0-b19f-5ea7-a21a-77cc584560d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436840} {:answer/id #uuid "db6f97ee-90ca-5331-9ef0-435bc5aba44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c868e06-3d4f-58da-ac1c-394649473371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436823} :answer-field/id #uuid "19000978-0267-5574-9f7e-9bbc7ec96a39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11bcd21b-33cd-5c67-b5f7-a5248fa0b15a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128090" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-2,0,4⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨7,-4,9⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-4,9⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 7+0\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f97420-5edb-586f-ab7a-66299006cada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21f97420-5edb-586f-ab7a-66299006cada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c02ae549-228a-54f0-a3ff-7af356d2a5aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ec4cd9-8af6-5de8-b3cc-526d00b07bfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-317344" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b),$ if $b\\times c=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ and $a=⟨1,4,2⟩.$\n\n$c\\cdot (a\\times b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-8+6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b)=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c37b847b-0603-5e0f-8973-9dd05a27d3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c37b847b-0603-5e0f-8973-9dd05a27d3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436839} :answer-field/id #uuid "56dee8e9-07f4-5b22-acca-8f6df3bf286f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "661d9816-4244-5782-9d68-b93f1424eb3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-317348" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨-2,5,4⟩$ and $a=⟨-1,3,2⟩.$\n\n$c\\cdot (b\\times a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2+15+8 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca56ef0-f2a8-56e7-a0f1-0c84b89b4f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436824} {:answer/id #uuid "10a95030-65da-5159-a9c2-11449ebd9b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436826} {:answer/id #uuid "159b7861-635b-50f0-91b9-d5cd93472fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436827} {:answer/id #uuid "a770a7ff-df6f-5dec-bec7-267dbaa53948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436837} {:answer/id #uuid "e3516f72-8edb-54cf-a1ba-115368557ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca56ef0-f2a8-56e7-a0f1-0c84b89b4f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436824} :answer-field/id #uuid "827eac35-448a-5c6d-a1b3-05ca38d592f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77263833-2d61-5397-981f-0a043abf2225" :question/math-academy-id "q-73451" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-5,2,3⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨1,-5,4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-5,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64225d0e-e8e7-5a62-ba67-a47c2125c90e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436832} {:answer/id #uuid "a490d505-52ab-5416-8a32-5429f938b745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436835} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ebf10e-c933-5ba3-a9e0-1a62a27771b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436838} {:answer/id #uuid "f98f5e82-18cb-5ae0-8ec5-0ba8c856e8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436845} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e0cc5c-df06-54c7-9fce-ac55f53eeb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64225d0e-e8e7-5a62-ba67-a47c2125c90e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436832} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e1a389-e227-565c-848f-f4e0c19bfd02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97c82ac3-b53e-55cf-b9b8-2b725636dcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-128093" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨5,-8,-7⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,1,2⟩,$ and $c=⟨-3,1,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨5,-8,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-6)+(-7)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27685b8e-75cd-51d4-8f57-2c5a8d85852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27685b8e-75cd-51d4-8f57-2c5a8d85852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436829} :answer-field/id #uuid "afdc93fa-0aca-5068-a46b-19029c20890d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2e5f62-97b7-5f13-893d-335d40132469" :question/math-academy-id "q-317306" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1,\\,4⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,3,\\,-5⟩.$\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,3,\\,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})(-2\\sqrt{2})+(-1)(3)+(4)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-3-20 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d1ac6eb-e3c3-5371-80e6-24c3f54f1da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d1ac6eb-e3c3-5371-80e6-24c3f54f1da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436831} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5b05a0-2b4a-5a26-9708-cfdb631cd43b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e30f2d7c-8431-5cce-a8b2-50fb65874116" :question/math-academy-id "q-317333" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $5a\\cdot ((-4b)\\times (-3c))=-120.$\n\n$b\\cdot (a\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a\\cdot ((-4b)\\times (-3c)) & =-120 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-120 \\\\ 60[a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-120 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\nb\\cdot (a\\times c)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a914c9-ac5b-510b-8770-498ebbd342d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436921} {:answer/id #uuid "88e03628-9e8e-5879-9ede-a96902f3797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07bd93-8453-5012-b970-faf3a1a28f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436927} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d3d9a8-1c50-5ad8-967c-67a2b8bdc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436928} {:answer/id #uuid "d674ed52-351d-5f4a-9c10-e0caa107251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4yz+2{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88e03628-9e8e-5879-9ede-a96902f3797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436926} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e44755-6b9a-5cca-b1ed-0c837e17c6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22c40f40-e4e5-529d-a83f-3f46f140c8db" :question/math-academy-id "q-105557" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{y}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,j+y{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nFind $\\text{div}\\,F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\  & =3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ab80cc-c857-5dfb-9a3a-cfdfd3d6fc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436922} {:answer/id #uuid "335bd84d-490e-5a69-979b-a11b772a9908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436923} {:answer/id #uuid "36f39977-9420-5792-a25e-40ed825c033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} {:answer/id #uuid "78e9e533-d5f7-5054-82d6-9d333edf1c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(1+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436925} {:answer/id #uuid "af0cee1b-58b3-5b9b-a7e7-5c521761b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(2-y-z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36f39977-9420-5792-a25e-40ed825c033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436924} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3222d-f787-5312-b008-17567e5ae0c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36837fe3-6724-5929-9249-1bb3cf0a5e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131175" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={e}^{2x}\\,i-y{e}^{2x}\\,j-z{e}^{2x}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-z{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93b12cef-0de3-5eae-b613-57a5a24bfe9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436968} {:answer/id #uuid "98177725-7d29-51e2-8e0c-74639d3d9e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436969} {:answer/id #uuid "9d796d42-9e19-53b0-bd6f-f39374f45098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436970} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e3648e-b68d-5ef6-9e9c-21d21e9cf0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436971} {:answer/id #uuid "d9abcc56-cbcc-55a7-98d1-f48f8a4fdcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d796d42-9e19-53b0-bd6f-f39374f45098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436970} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e485133-85f6-59c3-b320-b44902b78672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f9560d7-409d-5ebf-b57a-a19bcb1f8e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161064" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0a5627965f71f6eff869a45b1b575b892a67ebba9b1ec969f5dc38ba84cb250.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(0,-3).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/75/7515fffda1580baa332df454e7fe53a60f70944215f13d83e0e2269a0101dba6.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =2\\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5011f4d0-0c40-5764-9ee2-dff49de39782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437261} {:answer/id #uuid "639601fb-cc44-52a3-bcb1-718c744fd6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437262} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb75712-99aa-5d77-a9d1-0d236df66a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437264} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} {:answer/id #uuid "f132c264-24c8-5558-941c-11fddb4def73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c152aa95-4d94-5d9c-b5bb-30ac91e12518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125ca586-3337-5120-876d-92e40e11dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437260} {:answer/id #uuid "ba33cf18-d667-5a40-9d16-6cf17e9bc306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437263} {:answer/id #uuid "dacbed10-7956-5d58-b766-2a05b8deb073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437266} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc660c-f7e4-5ae5-93dd-d34894e5734f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437267} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269} :answer-field/id #uuid "f729b4c9-529c-5fb2-a0e6-3a129de28dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9cce6f-4b79-504d-bebd-6e1b2e2dc75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170671" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc93cb5-dc56-5ce8-9e29-5d04cbab18f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441593} {:answer/id #uuid "590d73f3-e82d-557d-94b6-037ec0668422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,880" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441594} {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} {:answer/id #uuid "753d4b97-5af5-5ac8-b2eb-b98600ccb71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441598} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e4fff1-a46e-50d1-90e7-531220bb1a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63245646-a801-5cdf-a8ba-6eca492c0f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e8d331-dfbd-5435-859b-6678685b6ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e02e690-e43f-5ecb-8751-7e2242b3bc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-126936" :question/problem "![](images/23/23d35daa15e11899592683f2f3215506cc0337358be843780dbf1190c0e38e2c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441591} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81656c-ec2b-52fd-99b0-096af9bbcd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441597} {:answer/id #uuid "e220a614-c017-51de-896b-5544add5e4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441606} {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbafbae6-9a9a-5e89-b7aa-04db6e10b82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441608} :answer-field/id #uuid "25134893-42bb-5ae1-bdf7-77b092bdf96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "311f57fc-3b6c-595d-a8b9-91e8d4d5d656" :question/math-academy-id "q-126947" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
